 + 15.11745 + 31.98982 + 34.96885 = 202.07612\n\nLet me try C8H14O3Cl:\n8(12.0000) + 14(1.00783) + 3(15.99491) + 34.96885 = 96 + 14.10962 + 47.98473 + 34.96885 = 193.0532\n\nAnd C8H15O3Cl:\n8(12.0000) + 15(1.00783) + 3(15.99491) + 34.96885 = 96 + 15.11745 + 47.98473 + 34.96885 = 194.07103\n\nC8H15O3Cl gives 194.071, which is very close to the observed 194.086. The small difference could be measurement error or I need to recalculate.\n\nActually, the M+1 peak intensity of 13.15% suggests approximately 12-13 carbons based on natural 13C abundance (~1.1% per carbon). But C8H15O3Cl only has 8 carbons, which would give ~8.8% for M+1. Let me reconsider.\n\nWith 13.15% M+1 intensity, this suggests about 12 carbons. But none of my C12 formulas with Cl fit the mass.\n\nLet me try without Cl and see if there's another explanation for the M+2 peak. Could it be two oxygens or sulfur?\n\nActually, the 32.79% M+2 peak is too large for anything other than Cl. Sulfur would give ~4.4%, and two oxygens would be negligible.\n\nGiven the strong Cl isotope pattern and the mass of 194.086, the best fit appears to be C8H15O3Cl with a calculated mass of 194.071.\n\nHowever, let me also consider if there might be a different ionization state. But ESI typically gives [M+H]+ or [M+Na]+ for positive mode.\n\nGiven all the calculations, C8H15O3Cl seems to be the most reasonable answer.\n</thought>\n<final_answer>C8H15O3Cl</final_answer>"
